// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// HOW TO RUN Lucky88Win
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// 1. From the project root, run:
//      ./singlish run examples/88_lucky88win.singlish
//
// 2. Open your browser and go to:
//      http://localhost:8089
//
// 3. Click "6 Numbers", "7 Numbers", or "8 Numbers" to choose how many to pick.
//
// 4. Click "Draw My Numbers!" to get your frequency-weighted hot numbers.
//
// HOW IT WORKS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Numbers are sampled randomly from a flat combined slice of all historical
// Singapore TOTO winning draws (2014–2026), with the full 1-49 set interspersed
// as a normaliser. Numbers that appeared more often in history occupy more
// positions in the combined slice and therefore have a proportionally higher
// chance of being picked — no complex maths, just natural frequency weighting.
//
// NOTE: This file is in .gitignore (personal/experimental).
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// CHANGES vs 88_lucky88.singlish (the original)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// 1. NUMBER GENERATION — core change
//    Before : rand.Intn(49) + 1  — pure uniform random from 1-49
//    After  : pickNumbers() samples random indices into buildTotoData(),
//             a combined flat []nombor of all historical TOTO draws.
//             Numbers drawn more often in history appear more times in the
//             combined slice → higher probability of being picked.
//
// 2. HISTORICAL DATA — new function buildTotoData()
//    Aggregates Singapore TOTO winning numbers from 2014 to Feb 2026:
//      toto2014, toto2015, toto2016, toto2017, toto2018, toto2019,
//      toto2020, toto2021, toto2022, toto2023, toto2024, toto2025, toto2026
//    Each year's draws are separated by totoSet (1-49 full set) as a
//    normaliser so no number can have zero weight.
//    Combined slice is ~7,000+ entries; rebuilt fresh each draw call.
//
// 3. PORT
//    Before : :8088
//    After  : :8089  (so both apps can run side-by-side)
//
// 4. BRANDING
//    Title    : "Lucky88Win - Historical TOTO Frequency Picker"
//    Subtitle : "Historical TOTO Frequency · 2014-2026"
//    Tagline  : "Hot from history! Huat ah! 🔥"
//    Footer   : mentions frequency weighting
//
// 5. RESULT LABEL
//    Before : "Your Lucky Numbers"
//    After  : "Your Hot Numbers"
//
// 6. GITIGNORE
//    examples/88_lucky88win.singlish is listed in .gitignore
//    (personal/experimental file — not committed to the repo)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// DEEP DIVE: Frequency-Weighted Historical Sampling
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ORIGINAL (pure random):
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   loop count(nums) < n {
//       got pick = rand.Intn(49) + 1   // uniform: every number 1-49 equally likely
//       nasi !seen[pick] {
//           nums = upsize(nums, pick)
//       }
//   }
//
//   Every number from 1 to 49 has an equal 1/49 chance each iteration.
//   No historical knowledge is used at all.
//
// NEW (frequency-weighted):
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   got data = buildTotoData()          // combined historical flat slice
//   got total = count(data)             // total entries (~7,000+)
//
//   loop count(nums) < n {
//       got idx  = rand.Intn(total)     // random position in the combined slice
//       got pick = data[idx]            // the number sitting at that position
//       nasi !seen[pick] {
//           nums = upsize(nums, pick)
//       }
//   }
//
//   Instead of picking a number directly (1-49), we pick a random INDEX into
//   the combined historical slice and read whatever number lives there.
//
// WHY THIS GIVES FREQUENCY WEIGHTING:
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   The combined slice is a flat list like:
//     [..., 32, 5, 46, 21, 32, 5, 32, ..., 1,2,3,...49, ..., 32, ...]
//
//   If number 32 appeared in 80 past draws it occupies ~80 positions.
//   If number 13 appeared in 40 past draws it occupies ~40 positions.
//   Plus each year is padded with the full totoSet (1-49) as a normaliser
//   so even the rarest number still has a baseline weight > 0.
//
//   Probability of picking number X in one iteration:
//     P(X) = count(X in combined slice) / total entries
//
//   Since frequently drawn numbers appear more times in the slice, they
//   have a proportionally higher P(X) — no sorting, no scoring, no maths.
//   The frequency bias emerges naturally from the data density.
//
// WORKED EXAMPLE (simplified):
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   Suppose combined = [5, 32, 5, 46, 5, 32, 1, 2, ..., 49]  (10 entries)
//   P(pick=5)  = 3/10 = 30%   ← appeared 3 times, hotter number
//   P(pick=32) = 2/10 = 20%
//   P(pick=46) = 1/10 = 10%   ← appeared once, cooler number
//
//   In reality the combined slice has ~7,000 entries spanning 13 years,
//   giving a smooth, data-driven probability distribution over 1-49.
//
// DEDUPLICATION:
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   The seen map ensures each drawn number is unique in the final result,
//   so we keep sampling (with replacement from the slice) until we have
//   n distinct numbers. Bias is preserved across iterations because we
//   always sample from the same full combined slice.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
